<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanchi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanchi is a hilltop Buddhist complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> whose Great Stupa, commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, was enlarged under the Shungas and given four carved gateways under the Satavahanas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanchi.jpg" alt="Sanchi" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stupa_1,_Sanchi_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to Irwin, the Sarnath and <b>Sanchi</b> pillar inscriptions were cut by inexperienced Indian engravers when stone engraving was still new in India, while the Sarnath capital was made under craftsmen from the former Achaemenid Empire.</blockquote><a href="/work/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Guilds took deposits, lent money and paid for monuments: an inscription credits the ivory carvers of Vidisha with work on the southern gateway of the great stupa at <b>Sanchi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li></ul></section>
